how are people going to go  it's absolutely right these are shocking  levels of increases  i represent wolverhampton southeast  we've got a lot of low-income families  in a constituency like that i've been on  the doorstep in recent weeks and people  are really worried about this they don't  know how they're going to pay and even  before  these risers have been announced people  have been so fearful they've been  turning their heating off even in very  cold weather  fearful about what's coming  and this is  just one part of a triple whammy that's  coming at households right now you've  got the energy price uh rises which  we're talking about today you've also  got tax rises coming in in april and  you've got declining real wages and  these three things are coming together  to squeeze household incomes in a way  that we haven't seen for many many years  doing what he can though isn't he well  the chancellor's come to this very very  late we've uh we've been calling  for action on energy prices for months  now we've been talking about cutting the  vat from energy prices and we produced a  fully costed package uh right at the  beginning of the year which would have  offered households 200 pounds  off the bill for everyone with another  400 pounds off for the poorest families  and we did that in a fully costed way  what's interesting about the speculation  about what the chancellor might announce  today is that he's not asking  the oil and gas companies who are making  the most out of all this to pay a single  penny towards this instead he's doing it  on a buy now pay later yeah although  it's the prime minister who said that  that's not going to happen but you know  the prime minister tells us lots of  things that are not going to happen and  then potentially they do is that the way  forward  well we just win full time when we  produced our package  we did it in a fully costed way because  if i'd come in here kay and said we're  going to do this and that on energy  bills the first question you'd have  asked me is how are you going to pay for  it  so we did that in a fully costed way by  contrast certainly as far as the  speculation goes  today the chancellor is going to  announce what is in effect a buy now pay  later scheme where he borrows the money  for this from taxpayers and asks them to  repay in future years  i've got no money and he doesn't ask the  oil and gas companies to pay a penny let  me give you an example shell have just  announced their recent profits they made  a profit of 20 billion dollars last year  the chief executive described it as a  momentous year  they're planning share buybacks and  increase dividends but they're not being  asked to pay a penny towards the package  so all roads are leading back to a  windfall tax is that what you're saying  well i think the package should be fully  costed and let me place this in a wider  context this week we've already learned  that 4.3 billion has been written off by  the government on covet fraud we've  learnt that almost nine billion has been  written off on ppe costs and now we have  a package that might cost five or six  billion where he doesn't ask for any  extra contribution towards it and you do  have to ask yourself why have the  conservatives become so complacent about  public money  well they would say that we're in the  middle of a pandemic because i put these  those points to um the uh to michael  gove yesterday all sorts of reasons he  gave to me for that but nevertheless let  me just ask you once more are we looking  is labor suggesting a windfall tax would  be the right way forward yes we've been  saying that for some weeks okay okay as  far as the prime minister is concerned  it's called a cream tea coo it's never  going to catch on it's not catchy at all  is it um  how much longer has he got  look i can't you know i can't guess this  boris johnson has been a survivor he's  always got out of scrapes usually by  making somebody else clean up the mess  