ell i  still think that whatever's happening  domestically we have respect through  having a strong military and we've got  to show not in a reckless way but we've  got to show that that military is there  that we stand in solidarity with our  allies nato is a solidarity alliance  that's what it is and we've got to make  that very clear especially to those  countries in eastern europe who are  members who'll be very fearful now when  they see that kind of russian aggression  i don't know if you've seen  our alex crawford's report on  afghanistan it was running  last night we can't actually show our  viewers until after nine o'clock because  it is so so distressing my goodness me  any parent watching and it is you know  you must watch it but it is absolutely  terrible to watch lots of children  particularly babies who are struggling  in afghanistan 22.8 million people  facing severe hunger 8.7 million are on  the brink of starvation  how can we have allowed this  humanitarian catastrophe to unfold  i haven't seen the report that you're  talking about but  i believe you know i've seen others  which which and you're absolutely right  in terms of how you  how you sum it up uh there are millions  of people on the edge of starvation  there are parents having to sell their  children  in order to try to buy food food for a  few hundred dollars the world must set  up  and and that too the world must set up  it must do more and  you know just because the taliban are  running that country we can't let the  people there starve so countries that  have the means to help  and we are in a position where we do  have some means to help together with  other similar countries i've got to make  sure the aid gets to the people there  the others running the country sure when  i when i put that to therese coffee last  week working pension secretary she said  we're doing our bit  look um  we've got to we can't just turn our  backs on this because  our military's pulled out because of the  change that happened in august uh  when you see millions of people in the  edge of starvation you've got to act in  concert with other countries who can  help to do so it would be the most  appalling  humanitarian catastrophe to let this get  any worse  but what level of responsibility should  we accept given that it was such a  chaotic withdrawal  well i don't see it so much as a sort of  responsibility in that way but it's a  responsibility in a human way when you  look at people who are starving whether  it's an economy that's just not  functioning you can hardly speak about a  functioning economy uh there at all and  whoever's fault all this is it's not the  fault of people in afghanistan who are  starving and that's why i ask the united  states france germany other countries  who have means have got to do more to  get help to people though okay it's good  to talk to you thanks for coming in much  appreciated

